course: english
lesson: 35
title: Direct speech: inverted commas and new speakers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Direct speech is the exact words a character says, and inverted commas (also called speech marks) hug those words like a speech bubble in a comic: "Hello," said Sam.
explain: The punctuation that ends the spoken words goes inside the marks: a comma if the sentence carries on ("Hello," said Sam), a question mark for a question ("Where are you going?" asked Mum), an exclamation mark for a shout ("Look out!" shouted Tom).
explain: Every time a new person speaks, start a new line, the way a play script gives each actor their own line; a whole conversation in one block is like everyone in class talking at once.
explain: Speech can be split in the middle: "If you're ready," said Mum, "we'll go." The second part starts with a small letter because it is still the same sentence.
explain: Inside the marks, characters talk their own way, dialect and slang included: "'Wee eejit,' said Da" (Belfast for "little idiot", said fondly) is perfectly punctuated, and it shows who Da is better than a description could.
fact: Direct speech is the exact words someone says; it goes inside inverted commas, also called speech marks.
fact: The punctuation that ends the spoken words goes inside the closing inverted commas.
fact: If the sentence continues after the speech, the spoken words end with a comma, not a full stop: "Hello," said Sam.
fact: Questions and exclamations in speech keep their ? or ! inside the marks: "Where are you going?" asked Mum.
fact: Start a new line (a new paragraph) for each new speaker.
fact: In split speech, commas go around the "said" part, and the second part starts with a small letter if it continues the same sentence.
fact: Indirect (reported) speech tells what someone said without their exact words and has no inverted commas: She said that she was tired.
fact: Both single ('...') and double ("...") inverted commas are correct; many UK books use single and US books double; be consistent.
fact: "Said" is often the best speech verb because readers hardly notice it; whispered, shouted or muttered add detail when used now and then.
fact: Dialect and slang can be written inside speech marks to show a character's voice.
fact: In Manchester "our kid" means my brother or sister and "dead boss" means really great; in Belfast "Da" is Dad and an "eejit" is an idiot.
exercise: Ella writes: Hello said Sam. Which is correct? | "Hello," said Sam. | "Hello" said Sam. | "Hello said Sam." | Hello, "said Sam."
exercise: Year 3: Where are you going asked Mum. Which is correct? | "Where are you going?" asked Mum. | "Where are you going," asked Mum? | "Where are you going" asked Mum? | Where are you going? "asked Mum."
exercise: Max writes a whole conversation between four people in one paragraph. What should he do? | Start a new line for each new speaker | Put all the speech in one giant pair of marks | Use a full stop after every word | Write it all in capital letters
exercise: Aoife from Belfast writes: 'Wee eejit,' said Da. Her teacher says "Correct punctuation!" Why? | The comma is inside the marks and the speech is exact | Dialect words never need any punctuation marks at all | The teacher didn't notice the slang | Single marks are wrong but allowed in Belfast
exercise: Year 4: correct this: 'Look out' shouted Tom. | 'Look out!' shouted Tom. | 'Look out'! shouted Tom. | 'Look out.' shouted Tom. | Look out! 'shouted Tom.'
exercise: Which split speech is punctuated correctly? | "If you're ready," said Mum, "we'll go." | "If you're ready," said Mum. "We'll go." | "If you're ready" said Mum "we'll go." | "If you're ready, said Mum, we'll go."
exercise: In the split speech above, why does "we'll" have a small letter? | It continues the same sentence | It is a contraction | Mum is speaking quietly | Speech never starts with a capital
exercise: Lucas uses "said" every single time. What is good advice? | Keep "said" mostly, and use others now and then | Never use "said" again, because it is far too boring | Use a different verb for every line | Leave out who is speaking altogether
exercise: Holly from Manchester writes: 'Our kid's dead boss,' she said. What does she mean? | My brother or sister is really great | My kid has died and is the boss | Our kid is really bossy and mean to everyone | The boss's child is very tired
exercise: Which sentence is punctuated correctly? | "Hi," said Sam. | "Hi" said Sam. | "Hi", said Sam. | "Hi said Sam."
exercise: Leo writes: She said "that she was tired." What is wrong? | Reported speech needs no inverted commas | It needs a question mark inside the closing marks | "Tired" needs a capital T | It needs two pairs of marks
exercise: Ruby asks "Single or double inverted commas?" What is the answer? | Either is fine if you are consistent | Only double, everywhere | Only single, everywhere | Single for questions and double for statements
exercise: Which is direct speech? | "I'm cold," said Ava. | Ava said that she was cold. | Ava felt cold and went in. | Ava told us she was cold.
exercise: Which speech verb shows the character is speaking very quietly? | whispered | bellowed | announced | yelled
exercise: How should this one sentence be punctuated: "We're going home" said Dad, sounding fed up? | A comma inside the marks after "home", a full stop at the end | A full stop after "home", outside the marks, and another after "Dad" | No punctuation is needed anywhere | A question mark after "Dad"
exercise: Which sentence turns direct speech into reported speech? | Mum said that tea was ready. | "Tea's ready," said Mum. | "Tea's ready!" shouted Mum. | "Is tea ready?" asked Mum.
test: Your mate goes "speech marks are for when people shout, innit." What is the truth? | They go around any exact spoken words | They are only for shouting in capital letters | They mean the words are made up | They are only for questions
test: ★★★ How many new lines are needed in a conversation where Sam speaks, then Mia, then Sam, then Sam again with no one in between? | Three | Four | Two | One
test: In a comic, a speech bubble says "Run!" What would the same line look like in a story? | "Run!" yelled the boy. | Run! "yelled the boy." | "Run" yelled the boy! | Run yelled! "the boy."
test: Nan from Cardiff, laughing: "Come here for a cwtch, you daft thing." ("Cwtch" is a cuddle in Wales.) Written as speech, where do the marks go? | Around all of Nan's exact words | Only around the Welsh word "cwtch" | Only around "you daft thing", the rude part | Nowhere, because it is Welsh
test: What would happen if every line of a long conversation was in one paragraph? | Readers would lose track of who was speaking | The story would become much easier for readers to follow | It would turn into reported speech | Nothing at all, readers always know
test: A Gen Z cousin writes: "I'm dead," she laughed. ("I'm dead" means that's so funny.) Is the punctuation right? | Yes, the comma is inside the marks | No, the comma must go outside the marks | No, slang can't go in speech marks | Yes, but only because she laughed
test: ★★★ Which sentence is split speech with correct punctuation? | "Wait," said Jo, "my lace is undone." | "Wait," said Jo, "My lace is undone." | "Wait" said Jo, "my lace is undone." | "Wait, said Jo, my lace is undone."
test: Dad, grumpy, says: Turn that racket down now (he is shouting). Which is best? | "Turn that racket down now!" bellowed Dad. | "Turn that racket down now?" bellowed Dad. | "Turn that racket down now," bellowed Dad? | Turn that racket down now! "bellowed Dad."
test: Tom from Texas: "Y'all fixin' to eat?" ("Fixin' to" means about to.) In a story, what goes at the end of his words? | A question mark inside the marks | A full stop outside the marks | A comma outside the marks | Nothing, it is dialect
test: ★★★ "Mia said that she would come." Which is the direct speech version? | "I will come," said Mia. | "She will come," said Mia. | Mia said, "she would come." | "Mia will come," said she would.
test: A UK book uses 'single marks' all through; then on page 40 it uses "double". What is the problem? | It is not consistent | Double marks are against the law | Single marks are always wrong | Nothing, it is better to mix
exam: Identify the correctly punctuated instance of direct speech. | "Sit down," said the teacher. | "Sit down" said the teacher. | "Sit down." said the teacher. | Sit down, "said the teacher."
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises indirect speech? | It reports the substance of what was said without inverted commas | It reproduces the speaker's exact words within inverted commas | It is employed solely for questions | It requires a new paragraph for each word
exam: It is given that a conversation involves two speakers alternating. State the convention in respect of layout. | A new line is commenced for each change of speaker | All utterances are placed in a single paragraph | Each utterance is placed in brackets | A colon precedes every utterance
exam: Select the response consistent with established convention concerning punctuation at the end of spoken words. | It is placed within the closing inverted commas | It is placed after the name of the speaker only | It is omitted entirely in all cases | It is placed before the opening inverted commas
